This section examines the concrete source code implementation across the critical 
modules of CodeMeet+. 
5.4.1  Client-Side Architecture & UI Component Tree 
The frontend is authored in TypeScript using React 18 functional components and hooks 
(`useState`, `useEffect`, `useRef`, `useCallback`). The routing hierarchy (`App.tsx`) 
protects authenticated endpoints via `ProtectedRoute.tsx`. The core views include 
`Landing.tsx`, `Login.tsx`, `Signup.tsx`, `Dashboard.tsx`, `Room.tsx`, and `Chat.tsx`. The 
following listing demonstrates client-side route protection and session state propagation: 
import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route, Navigate } from 'react-router-dom'; 
import { AuthProvider, useAuth } from './context/AuthContext'; 
import Dashboard from './pages/Dashboard'; 
import Room from './pages/Room'; 
import Chat from './pages/Chat'; 
import Login from './pages/Login'; 

const ProtectedRoute = ({ children }: { children: JSX.Element }) => { 
  const { user, loading } = useAuth(); 
  if (loading) return <div className='flex h-screen items-center justify-
center'>Loading...</div>; 
  return user ? children : <Navigate to='/login' replace />; 
}; 

export default function App() { 
  return ( 
    <AuthProvider> 
      <BrowserRouter> 
        <Routes> 
          <Route path='/' element={<Navigate to='/dashboard' replace />} /> 
          <Route path='/login' element={<Login />} /> 
          <Route path='/dashboard' element={<ProtectedRoute><Dashboard 
/></ProtectedRoute>} /> 
          <Route path='/room/:id' element={<ProtectedRoute><Room 
/></ProtectedRoute>} /> 
          <Route path='/chat' element={<ProtectedRoute><Chat /></ProtectedRoute>} 
/> 
        </Routes> 
      </BrowserRouter> 
    </AuthProvider> 
  ); 
} 
5.4.2  WebRTC PeerManager & Supabase Signaling Protocol 
To manage multi-peer WebRTC connections cleanly, a standalone `PeerManager` class 
(`src/lib/webrtc.ts`) manages the lifecycle of `RTCPeerConnection` instances in a 
dictionary mapping `peerId -> RTCPeerConnection`. Figure 5.3 depicts the exact 
signaling handshake.


29 

Figure 5.3: WebRTC Mesh Signaling Protocol via Supabase Realtime 
To prevent duplicate offer collisions when two users join simultaneously, CodeMeet+ 
implements deterministic asymmetric initiation: `if (p.user_id !== user.id && p.user_id < 
user.id) { peerMgrRef.current.callPeer(p.user_id); }`. The user with the lexically smaller 
UUID creates the offer, while the other awaits the incoming signal. Below is the core 
implementation of `PeerManager`: 
export class PeerManager { 
  meetingId: string; selfId: string; localStream: MediaStream | null = null; 
  peers: Map<string, RTCPeerConnection> = new Map(); 
  onRemote: RemoteStreamHandler; channel: any = null; 

  async start(localStream: MediaStream) { 
    this.localStream = localStream; 
    this.channel = supabase.channel(`signals:${this.meetingId}:${this.selfId}`) 
      .on('postgres_changes', { event: 'INSERT', schema: 'public', table: 
'signals', 
        filter: `to_user=eq.${this.selfId}` }, (payload) => 
this.handleSignal(payload.new)) 
      .subscribe(); 
  } 
  async callPeer(peerId: string) { 
    const pc = this.createPC(peerId); 
    const offer = await pc.createOffer(); 
    await pc.setLocalDescription(offer); 
    await this.sendSignal(peerId, { type: 'offer', sdp: offer }); 
  } 
} 
5.4.3  Monaco Code Editor Realtime State Synchronization


30 
In `Room.tsx`, the Monaco editor is instantiated with dynamic language syntax binding. 
To prevent excessive network dispatch on every keystroke, code changes are debounced 
by 600 ms before executing an UPDATE query against `meetings.code_content`. 
Furthermore, non-administrative members receive `readOnly: !canEdit` within Monaco's 
editor options, preventing DOM edits while keeping syntax selection enabled. The 
debounced persistence hook is implemented as follows: 
// Save code (debounced 600ms) — only host/admin 
useEffect(() => { 
  if (!canEdit || !id || codeChangeFromRemote.current) { 
    codeChangeFromRemote.current = false; 
    return; 
  } 
  const t = setTimeout(() => { 
    supabase.from('meetings').update({ code_content: code, language }).eq('id', 
id); 
  }, 600); 
  return () => clearTimeout(t); 
}, [code, language, canEdit, id]); 
5.4.4  Remote Code Execution Pipeline (Piston Integration) 
The execution pipeline is triggered when an authorized user clicks 'Run Code'. The React 
client invokes the serverless edge function `supabase.functions.invoke('run-code', { body: 
{ language, code, stdin } })`. The edge function maps the requested language to Piston's 
runtime dictionary (`python -> 3.10.0`, `java -> 15.0.2`, `cpp -> 10.2.0`), submits the 
payload to `https://emkc.org/api/v2/piston/execute`, and aggregates `stdout`, `stderr`, and 
compilation logs into a unified output buffer: 
Deno.serve(async (req) => { 
  const { language, code, stdin } = await req.json(); 
  const cfg = LANG_MAP[language]; 
  const r = await fetch('https://emkc.org/api/v2/piston/execute', { 
    method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, 
    body: JSON.stringify({ language: cfg.language, version: cfg.version, 
      files: [{ name: cfg.filename, content: code }], stdin: stdin || '', 
      compile_timeout: 10000, run_timeout: 5000 }) 
  }); 
  const data = await r.json(); 
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ 
    output: (data.run?.stdout || '') + (data.run?.stderr ? '\n' + data.run.stderr 
: ''), 
    exitCode: data.run?.code ?? 0 
  })); 
}); 
5.4.5  Contextual AI Coding Assistant & Event Streaming 
The AI copilot component (`AIChatPanel.tsx`) opens as a slide-out sheet. It 
communicates with the `ai-chat` Edge Function via HTTP POST, transmitting prior chat


